    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Candomblé venerates spirits called orixás, inkice or vodun, who are held to be subservient to a distant creator god, Olodumaré, and each person is believed to have a tutelary orixá who informs their personality.</li><li>It has no central authority or sacred text: it is organised around autonomous terreiros led by a mãe or pai de santo, and divided into nations, of which the Ketu, Jeje and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> are the most prominent.</li><li>Its practitioners drum, sing and dance so that an orixá may possess one of them, and the religion has met discrimination, being labelled devil worship by some critics and attacked by Evangelical opponents in the 2000s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Every initiate is given a new name, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a> de santo, which usually indicates the identity of their tutelary orixá, and receives a string of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> coloured for that orixá: white for Oxalá, dark blue for Ogum, red and white for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shango">Xangô</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

